    {
      "id": "art-1",
      "authorId": "system",
      "authorName": "RVCYBER Threat Research",
      "authorUsername": "rvcyber_sec",
      "authorAvatar": "",
      "title": "Understanding SQL Injection: From Syntax Breakdown to Prepared Statements",
      "slug": "understanding-sql-injection-syntax-breakdown",
      "summary": "A deep-dive technical guide into how relational databases interpret unfiltered input, blind vs error-based SQLi, and modern parameterized queries.",
      "content": "## What is SQL Injection (SQLi)?\n\nSQL Injection occurs when untrusted user input is directly concatenated into a dynamic SQL query without validation or parameterization. This allows an attacker to alter the query logic executed by the database engine.\n\n```sql\n-- Vulnerable Dynamic Query:\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' AND password = '' OR '1'='1';\n```\n\n### Safe Defense: Parameterized Queries\n\nAlways use prepared statements where variables are treated as literal data, never executable syntax:\n\n```typescript\n// Safe parameterized query in modern Node.js / PostgreSQL:\nconst query = 'SELECT id, email, role FROM users WHERE username = $1 AND status = $2';\nconst result = await db.query(query, [inputUsername, 'active']);\n```\n\n### Key Takeaways\n1. Never concatenate raw strings into SQL statements.\n2. Employ least privilege principles for database service credentials.\n3. Validate and enforce type constraints at application boundaries.",
      "category": "Web Security",
      "tags": [
        "OWASP",
        "SQLi",
        "Defense",
        "AppSec"
      ],
      "isPublished": true,
      "viewsCount": 342,
      "likesCount": 58,
      "createdAt": "2026-10-08T06:56:18.148Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-08T06:56:18.148Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "art-2",
      "authorId": "system",
      "authorName": "Defense Matrix Labs",
      "authorUsername": "matrix_analyst",
      "authorAvatar": "",
      "title": "SOC Analyst Incident Response Playbook: Investigating Suspicious PowerShell Execution",
      "slug": "soc-incident-response-powershell-analysis",
      "summary": "Practical steps for Security Operations Center triage when Windows Event ID 4104 flags encoded base64 PowerShell commands.",
      "content": "## Event Log Analysis for Windows Endpoint Triage\n\nWhen monitoring endpoints via Sysmon or Windows Event Forwarding, PowerShell Script Block Logging (Event ID 4104) provides full visibility into decoded commands.\n\n### Triage Workflow:\n1. **Identify Parent Process:** Was powershell.exe spawned by cmd.exe, winword.exe, or an administrative scheduler?\n2. **Decode EncodedCommand:** Extract the Base64 string and decode Unicode bytes safely in an isolated sandbox.\n3. **Network Telemetry:** Check firewall and proxy logs for external IP sockets created within 30 seconds of execution.\n\n```powershell\n# Safe Sandbox decoding of suspicious script block:\n$encoded = \"V3JpdGUtSG9zdCAnaGVsbG8n\"\n[System.Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetString([System.Convert]::FromBase64String($encoded))\n```\n\n### Remediation\nIsolate the machine from the network subnet, revoke active Kerberos tickets, and collect volatile memory dump using FTK Imager.",
      "category": "SOC & Incident Response",
      "tags": [
        "SOC",
        "BlueTeam",
        "PowerShell",
        "WindowsSecurity"
      ],
      "isPublished": true,
      "viewsCount": 215,
      "likesCount": 39,
      "createdAt": "2026-10-09T06:56:18.148Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-09T06:56:18.148Z"
    }
